solution:
The chain is a row of six arrow-shaped boxes, left to right: Exploration/Production - Transport - Storage - Trading/Sales - Distribution - Services.

The three brackets above them:
- 'upstream' covers exploration/production and reaches into transport, i.e. finding the gas, producing it and getting it off the field;
- 'midstream' spans transport, storage and trading/sales, i.e. everything that moves the molecule and the title between the field and the local market;
- 'downstream' covers distribution and services, i.e. the low-pressure network and everything sold to the end customer - metering, billing, appliance service.

The brackets overlap deliberately: transport is claimed by both upstream and midstream, and trading/sales sits at the boundary of midstream and downstream, because the same physical asset can be classified differently depending on who owns it. What matters for the exam is the order of the stages and the fact that the regulated natural monopolies - transport and distribution - sit in the middle and at the end, whereas production and trading are competitive.

Definition: natural gas means any hydrocarbon or mixture of hydrocarbons and other gases consisting primarily of methane which, at a temperature of 15 degrees Celsius and at atmospheric pressure, is predominantly in the gaseous state. Two elements matter - the composition (primarily methane) and the reference condition (15 °C and atmospheric pressure), because the reference condition is what separates the gas from the liquids that come out of the same well.

To be distinguished from natural gas: town gas (coke oven gas), refinery gas, other 'technical' gases and biogas. These are combustible gases too, but they have a different composition, a different calorific value and a different Wobbe index, so they cannot simply be fed into a natural gas network.

The related products:
- LNG, liquefied natural gas (GNL in Italian and French) - the same methane, liquefied for shipping;
- NGL, natural gas liquids - the heavier hydrocarbons, a byproduct of oil and gas production, transported like oil;
- LPG, liquefied petroleum gas - propane and butanes, separated partially before liquefaction;
- biomethane, i.e. 'bio-natural gas' - biogas upgraded until it is compatible with natural gas and can be injected into the network;
- SNG, synthetic natural gas - a natural gas-like gas formed by cracking liquid hydrocarbons.
Condensate is used in refineries for blending with heavy crude oil and as a raw material for chemical production.

All four use the same pressure, 1.01325 * 10^5 Pa (= 101.325 kPa = 1.01325 bar); they differ only in temperature:
- Norm condition: 273.15 K = 0 °C - Germany, Denmark, Austria;
- Standard (European): 288.15 K = 15 °C - Italy, United Kingdom;
- Standard (US): 288.71 K = 60 °F - US, Middle East, Africa, Latin America;
- Basic: 293.15 K = 20 °C - Russia and Eastern Europe.

Calculation. For a fixed amount of gas at fixed pressure the ideal gas law gives V ~ T, so
V(15 °C) / V(0 °C) = 288.15 K / 273.15 K = 1.0549.

The same quantity of gas is therefore 5.5 % more cubic metres when it is counted at 15 °C than when it is counted at 0 °C. Turned around: a cubic metre measured under the European standard condition contains 5.2 % less energy than a cubic metre measured under the German norm condition.

Why this is examinable: gas is traded by volume but paid for by energy. Booking a cross-border deal in 'm3' without stating the reference condition creates an error of several per cent - larger than the trading margin. The safe habit is to convert everything to kWh immediately, and to write Nm3 (norm) or Sm3 (standard) explicitly.

Note a slip on the slide: the column is headed 'Pressure [kPa]' but the entry 1.01325 * 10^5 is the pressure in Pa; in kPa it would be 101.325.

Main uses:
- heating of residential and commercial spaces;
- (industrial) process heat energy;
- electric power generation;
- chemical processes;
- fuel for vehicles;
- a long tail of other applications - the 'natural gas-supplied house', clothes dryers, fireplaces and tile stoves, patio heaters, gas heat pumps, diffusion-absorption heat pumps, fuel cells and absorption refrigeration.

The three chemical processes:
1. Ammonia synthesis in the Haber-Bosch process, the basis of nitrogen fertilizer. Gas is used twice here: as the source of the hydrogen and as the energy for the process.
2. Iron ore reduction in the blast furnace process, where the gas (or the hydrogen made from it) takes the oxygen out of the ore - the direct-reduction route that is meant to replace coke.
3. Production of hydrogen, today overwhelmingly by steam reforming of methane.

These are hardest to replace because in them natural gas is a feedstock, not a fuel. In heating and in power generation the gas only delivers energy, and any other energy carrier - electricity from a heat pump, wind, solar - can deliver the same energy. In ammonia synthesis and in ore reduction the methane supplies the hydrogen atoms themselves, so it can only be substituted by another source of hydrogen, i.e. by electrolysis. That is exactly why the hydrogen chapters of the lecture matter to industry and why fertilizer and steel are the first candidates for green hydrogen.

Sketch: gas year on the horizontal axis, running from 1.10 (1 October) through the winter to 1.9 (1 September). The daily off-take is drawn as a jagged mountain: low at the start of October, rising through November and December, peaking in January/February, falling away through spring, reaching its minimum in July/August and rising again in September. The jaggedness is the weather - each spike is a cold day.

The three quantities in the figure:
- quantity is the area under the curve, i.e. the energy actually taken over a period, measured in kWh or m3;
- flow is the height of the curve at a given moment, i.e. the rate of off-take, measured in kWh/h or m3/h;
- capacity is the horizontal line drawn above the highest peak: the maximum flow that has been booked and that the network must be able to deliver, in kWh/h or m3/h.
Capacity is dimensioned by the single worst hour, not by the annual quantity - that is the entire economics of gas transport in one picture.

The contract quantities, all measured as quantity, not as capacity:
- ACQ - annual contract quantity, the volume over the whole gas year;
- QCQ - quarterly contract quantity;
- MCQ - monthly contract quantity;
and below them, not in this figure, the DCQ, the daily contract quantity. The nesting in the figure shows the hierarchy: MCQ inside QCQ inside ACQ.

The profile is called temperature-correlated because the off-take follows the ambient temperature almost linearly below the heating limit - it is the household and small commercial load, and it is what makes seasonal storage necessary.
